  "caveats": [
    "1. **Definition of `bsgs = false`.** The giant rotation is not shared; the weights keep the same pre-rotated (BSGS) layout and the same Galois keys. Every product with a nonzero giant shift is finished (relinearized) and rotated on its own; the products of the zero-shift block are summed as with BSGS. Per matvec of dimension `d` (offset 0): `d - 1` rotations, of which only the `baby - 1` rotations of `x` can be hoisted; with `lazy_relin` `1 + d - baby` relins, without it `d` ([`EvalConfig::matvec_rotations`], [`EvalConfig::matvec_enc_relins`]). At `d = 16` (`baby = giant = 4`), as (hoistable rotations / individual rotations / relins): | form | hoistable rot. | individual rot. | relins (lazy) | relins (not lazy) | |---|---|---|---|---| | default BSGS | 3 | 3 | 4 | 16 | | `bsgs = false` (this library) | 3 | 12 | 13 | 16 | | textbook diagonal method (not implemented) | 15 | 0 | 1 | 16 | The textbook (Halevi-Shoup) diagonal method rotates `x` by all `d - 1` steps (all hoistable) and sums the products lazily; it needs un-pre-rotated diagonals, i.e. another weight layout and gradient form, so it is not implemented. The all-off configuration has the same counts in both forms (15 rotations, 16 relins at `d = 16`).",
    "2. **Bias.** Against the strongest naive baseline (the textbook method with hoisting and lazy relinearization), the cost of the `bsgs = false` rows overstates what BSGS gains: the measured BSGS gain is an **upper bound**. Conversely, the marginal gains of `hoist` and `lazy_relin` measured inside `bsgs = false` configurations are **lower bounds** (only `baby - 1` rotations can be hoisted and only the zero-shift block summed lazily: e.g. `lazy_relin` saves 3 relins at `d = 16` instead of 15).",
    "3. **`hoist` changes no [`OpCounter`] value.** The counter does not distinguish hoisted from individual rotations, so the effect of `hoist` shows only in wall-clock time, never in key-switch counts.",
    "4. **`bsgs` does not touch the gradient's outer product** (`Dense::weight_grad_scaled`, `Dense::weight_grad_reduced`): its `(baby - 1) + (giant - 1)` input rotations are what the pre-rotated weight layout requires, not a BSGS choice; only `hoist` changes how they run. The interactive gradient multiplies each diagonal with one [`Evaluator::mul`] (nothing to sum lazily), so `lazy_relin` does not change it either."
  ],
